{
  "id": "Compound::DB00327 - Gene::1576",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that Hydromorphone may be associated with Cyp3a4 through a series of molecular interactions involving opioid receptor agonists. Hydromorphone, an opioid, is linked to Ureteral Spasm, Breakthrough Pain, and Cheyne-Stokes Respiration, all of which are mediated by Morphine, an opioid receptor agonist. Morphine binds to Cyp3a4, indicating a potential mechanism by which opioid agonists influence the enzyme's activity. The lowest common ancestors of Hydromorphone and Morphine are both Opioid Receptor Agonists and Morphinane Alkaloids, suggesting a shared pathway in their interaction with Cyp3a4. The scores from the paths indicate a moderate level of confidence in this relationship, with some variation in the strength of the evidence across different pathways. The integration of these findings suggests that Hydromorphone may exert a modulatory effect on Cyp3a4 through its interaction with opioid receptor agonists, potentially influencing drug metabolism.",
